# Build context for the admin image, shipped with the Dockerfile so every
# consumer gets the same slim upload:
#
#   fly deploy -c fly.admin.toml --ignorefile hydra/docker/admin.Dockerfile.dockerignore
#
# The filename matters: BuildKit picks up `<dockerfile-path>.dockerignore`
# automatically, so this one name serves BOTH a local `docker build -f
# hydra/docker/admin.Dockerfile .` and the explicit --ignorefile that flyctl
# needs (flyctl builds the context itself and does not apply the convention).
# Named `admin.dockerignore` it would have worked only for fly, and a local
# build would have silently used the repo's shared .dockerignore instead.
#
# admin.Dockerfile COPYs one thing -- ${INKA_PATH}/ -- so everything else in a
# consumer's repo was being uploaded and discarded on every deploy. In
# one consumer that was 134 MB across 3,634 files, of which its Plone
# distribution (72 MB) and its frontend (40 MB) are unreachable from any
# instruction here. Narrowing it took that context to 15.55 MB.
#
# This is an ALLOW-LIST (`*` then `!path`), so a new top-level directory in any
# consumer stays out of the upload until something names it. A shared, per-repo
# .dockerignore cannot do that: it has to satisfy every image at once, which is
# how api/ and frontend/ ended up in the admin context.
#
# Consumers vendor Inka at different paths, so all the known ones are allowed;
# the ones a given repo does not have simply match nothing. Paths below are
# spelled out per location rather than written `**/examples`, because a `**`
# glob would also match docs/examples -- see the warning on that directory.
*
!hydra
!inka
!frontend/inka
!frontend/hydra

# Re-cloned by mrs-developer or rebuilt by razzle inside the image.
hydra/core
hydra/build
hydra/cache
inka/core
inka/build
inka/cache
frontend/inka/core
frontend/inka/build
frontend/inka/cache
frontend/hydra/core
frontend/hydra/build
frontend/hydra/cache

# The example frontends. Checked before excluding: these are NOT pnpm workspace
# members -- Inka's pnpm-workspace.yaml globs `examples/*` and then negates all
# three that exist (hydra-vue-f7, hydra-nextjs, nuxt-blog-starter). A missing
# workspace member fails --frozen-lockfile, so this exclusion is load-bearing
# and so is its precision.
#
# docs/ is deliberately NOT excluded, and must never be: the members
# docs/examples/test-{react,svelte,vue,astro} live inside it. Writing this rule
# as `**/examples` would match docs/examples too and break the install.
hydra/examples
inka/examples
frontend/inka/examples
frontend/hydra/examples

# Tests, reports and notes the image never runs.
hydra/tests-playwright
hydra/playwright-report
hydra/test-results
hydra/_internal-docs
hydra/superpowers
inka/tests-playwright
inka/playwright-report
inka/test-results
inka/_internal-docs
inka/superpowers
frontend/inka/tests-playwright
frontend/inka/playwright-report
frontend/inka/test-results
frontend/hydra/tests-playwright
frontend/hydra/playwright-report
frontend/hydra/test-results

**/node_modules
**/.git
**/__pycache__
